import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout title="Autoencoders / Obsession 01 / Adam Thorne" description="Autoencoders: a saved interactive experiment with drawing representations and possible completions" obsessions>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="site-header" id="top"><a class="home" href="/">Adam Thorne</a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/obsessions/">← Current obsessions</a></nav>
</header>
    <main id="main">
      <div class="obsessions-intro"><p class="obsession-kicker">SAVED ENTRY 01 / OCTOBER 2026</p>
<h1>Autoencoders</h1>
<a class="text-link" href="/obsessions/">Back to the obsessions <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <section class="technical-obsession" id="autoencoders" aria-labelledby="autoencoders-title">
  <div class="obsession-copy">
    <p class="obsession-kicker">THE EXPERIMENT</p>
    <h2 id="autoencoders-title">What the drawing<br>becomes</h2>
    <p>What does a model keep when it has to squeeze a drawing into just 32 values?</p>
    <p>I'm interested in that representation: what gets kept, what gets lost, and how it can help predict what comes next.</p>
    <p>This demo reads a digit as you draw. The encoder state feeds a guess and a possible finished drawing. Try it before you've finished the last stroke.</p>
    <details class="model-notes"><summary>A little more about the model</summary><p>A convolutional encoder turns the drawing into 32 activation values. A classifier reads that state to guess the digit; a decoder uses the state and the class probabilities to generate a possible finish.</p>
<p>The squares show the actual activations, rather than assigned features such as “loop” or “line”. A finish is a prediction, and partial handwriting can be ambiguous.</p>
</details>
    <a class="text-link" href="/obsessions/#archive">Back to the archive <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </div>
  <figure class="digit-figure" aria-labelledby="digit-title">
          <div class="digit-heading"><h3 id="digit-title">Draw a number</h3>
<span>It guesses as you go</span></div>
          <div class="digit-machine">
            <div class="input-column">
              <div class="drawing-sheet">
                <span class="sheet-label">ONE DIGIT, 0–9</span>
                <canvas id="digit-canvas" width="280" height="280" aria-label="Draw a single digit here using a mouse or touch. The guess, possible finish, and encoder state update while you draw. You can also use the Show me one button.">Use Show me one to try a sample digit.</canvas>
                <button class="drawing-hint" id="drawing-hint" type="button" aria-label="Clear drawing and try your own">Try your own ↗</button>
              </div>
              <div class="encoder-panel">
                <div class="encoder-heading"><span>Inside the encoder</span><span>32 values</span></div>
                <div id="encoder-state" class="state-grid" role="group" aria-label="Encoder values; select a square to edit it"></div>
                <details id="state-editor" class="state-editor">
                  <summary>Play with the values</summary>
                  <div class="state-slider-heading"><label for="state-slider">Value <span id="state-dimension">1</span></label><output id="state-slider-value" for="state-slider">—</output></div>
                  <input id="state-slider" type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0" aria-describedby="state-status state-help" disabled>
                  <p id="state-status" class="state-status" role="status">Draw a number to explore its values</p>
                  <button id="state-restore" type="button" disabled>Restore drawing values <span aria-hidden="true">↺</span></button>
                  <p id="state-help" class="state-help">Pick any square, then move the slider to see what the model decodes on the right</p>
                </details>
                <details class="state-explanation"><summary>What am I looking at?</summary><p>Each square shows one value in the model's shared representation of your drawing. Darker squares are more active. Select a square to change its value, or read the full vector below. Both predictions use this same state. Edited values are decoded by the model and may no longer describe your original drawing. The squares aren't assigned meanings like “a loop” or “a line”.</p>
<output id="encoder-values" class="state-values" aria-label="Raw encoder values">Waiting for a drawing</output></details>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="guess-slip">
              <span class="guess-label">MY GUESS</span>
              <strong id="digit-guess">7</strong>
              <span id="digit-confidence">Reading your handwriting</span>
              <span class="runner-label">Other guesses</span>
              <span id="digit-alternatives">—</span>
              <div class="slip-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              <span class="finish-label">Possible finish</span>
              <canvas id="completion-canvas" width="112" height="112" role="img" aria-label="A predicted finished digit generated from the encoder state">A possible finished drawing.</canvas>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="digit-controls"><button id="digit-clear" type="button">Start over <span aria-hidden="true">↺</span></button><button id="digit-example" type="button">Show me one <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button></div>
          <figcaption>An encoder reads your strokes and predicts a possible finish</figcaption>
          <p class="sr-only" id="digit-announcement" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to try the drawing demo.</p>
</noscript>
        </figure>
</section>


    </main>
    <footer class="site-footer"><a href="/">Back to the portfolio ↗</a><span>Adam Thorne</span><a href="#top" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></footer>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
